{"id": "sevt_01Tu3eXkm6bK4JF4M47hEXVS", "content": [{"text": "**What happened:** Checkout p99 latency went from about 75 ms to about 3,850 ms at 14:31, right after the checkout deploy at 14:31:18. By 14:40 the checkout DB connection pool was exhausted (50/50 active, 12 waiters). From then on requests failed with `PoolTimeout` 500s, and error rate rose from 0 to 0.2\u20130.6.\n\n**Timeline:**\n- **14:31:** Deploy `a3f9c21` (checkout, \"refactor order summary builder for readability\"). p99 jumps in the same minute.\n- **14:40:** Pool saturation warnings (42\u201346/50) on checkout-1 and checkout-2, then pool exhaustion and the first 500s on checkout-1 and checkout-3.\n- **14:55:** The inventory deploy `7b3e8f0` came after the incident began, so it isn't the cause.\n\n**Mechanism:** The diff replaces a single batched query (`order_id__in=ids`, grouped in memory) with a loop that runs one `OrderItem` query per open order. Each request now holds a DB connection for N sequential queries instead of one. That slows every request, which holds connections longer, and at 14:40 the pool ran out.\n\n**Log check:** The log has 3,320 lines of the new per-row query `SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ?`. That is the signature of the N+1 pattern.\n\n**Fix:** Revert `a3f9c21`, or restore the batched query. The readability refactor can be redone while keeping the batch.\n\n> **Root cause:** `a3f9c21` \u2014 the refactor turned a single batched `order_items` query into one query per order (N+1), which slowed checkout from 14:31 and exhausted the DB connection pool at 14:40.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T05:44:40.872991Z", "type": "agent.message"}
